{"id":"ec8661a1-9c66-489b-9da1-37ad5bc6b948","object":"chat.completion","created":1784794903,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `ddmin_with_unresolved` as `solve(case)`. The task is to perform delta debugging (ddmin) but with an extra state: UNRESOLVED. The problem description: \"Minimize a failure-inducing ordered configuration using the registered PASS, FAIL, and UNRESOLVED semantics and exact subset/complement schedule.\" We have a deterministic oracle fixture? The function `solve` returns a JSON-serializable object. The fixture gives cases with `items`, `required_failure_items`, `unresolved_without`. So the oracle is based on these fields: given a set of items (the configuration), we can determine if it's FAIL, PASS, or UNRESOLVED based on the presence of required_failure_items and unresolved_without. The case payload includes `items` (list of all items, presumably in order, but order may matter? The description says \"ordered configuration\", but the items are listed as strings. The ddmin algorithm typically works on a sequence/set; here it might be a sequence because of \"ordered configuration\". The \"exact subset/complement schedule\" refers to the ddmin algorithm: at each granularity, try subsets; if any subset fails, reduce to that subset; else try complements; if any complement fails, reduce to that complement; else increase granularity.\n\nWe need to implement ddmin that returns a 1-minimal failure (i.e., minimal set that still fails, with no proper subset that fails, but subsets that are unresolved are considered? The spec says \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So we reduce only when we find a subset that fails. If a subset is UNRESOLVED, we cannot reduce? But the standard ddmin algorithm: when testing a subset, if result is FAIL, we replace current configuration with that subset. If all subsets are PASS (or UNRESOLVED?), we then test complements. The \"unresolved_semantics\" and \"registered_call_order\" are important. The specification says \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So if a subset is UNRESOLVED, we do not reduce, but do we continue trying other subsets? And for complements: if a complement fails, we reduce to complement. If complement is UNRESOLVED? Probably not reduce.\n\nWe need to infer the oracle: given a set of items (a configuration), determine FAIL, PASS, or UNRESOLVED based on the items and the fields `required_failure_items` and `unresolved_without`. How? The description: \"The attached public fixtures define input shapes only.\" The payload includes `items` (all possible items), `required_failure_items` (a list of items that must be present to cause failure?), `unresolved_without` (a list of items, maybe if those are absent, the test is unresolved?). The case names and fixtures suggest this is for SE-DD-01, a task in the EffectSlice framework? We need to deduce the oracle logic.\n\nFrom the cases:\n\nCase 000: items = [d0..d7], required_failure_items = [d2,d3], unresolved_without = []. Likely failure condition: test FAIL if the set contains all items in required_failure_items? Or perhaps test FAIL if the set contains exactly required_failure_items? But \"failure-inducing ordered configuration\" – minimization of a configuration (set/sequence) that causes failure. So the oracle might be: if the configuration (subset of items) contains all required_failure_items, then FAIL; else if the configuration does not contain any of the unresolved_without items (i.e., missing some items that make test unresolved), then UNRESOLVED; else PASS.\n\nIn case 000, unresolved_without empty, so no UNRESOLVED states; only FAIL if contains d2,d3, else PASS. In case 001: required_failure_items = [d3,d4], unresolved_without = [d0]. So if configuration contains both d3 and d4? But also, if configuration does NOT contain d0, maybe it's unresolved. Let's think: \"unresolved_without\" – the test is unresolved if the configuration does not include these items? The word \"without\" suggests that if those items are absent, the test cannot be resolved. So if the configuration does not contain any of the items in unresolved_without, the result is UNRESOLVED. Else, if it does contain all required_failure_items, then FAIL. Otherwise PASS.\n\nIn case 002: required_failure_items=[d4,d7], unresolved_without=[d0]. So similar.\n\nThus oracle:\n- Input: case.payload, and a subset of items (configuration).\n- Let C be the set of items in config.\n- If any item in unresolved_without is missing from C (i.e., unresolved_without is not subset of C"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":844,"completion_tokens":1024,"total_tokens":1868,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":768},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1024},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":768,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":76},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}